From ff46de9df13ab9ef8fc69397814a3129f06bceb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Wolfe <rnwolfe@google.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 21:31:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] bgpd: send no label with an EVPN gateway IP nexthop

A path imported from an EVPN route carries the VNI as its label, in the
3-octet EVPN encoding. bgp_zebra_announce_parse_nexthop() sends it to
zebra as the VNI when the nexthop is an EVPN one (ZAPI_NEXTHOP_FLAG_EVPN),
and as an MPLS label when bgp_is_valid_label() says it is one. A path
with a gateway IP overlay index (RFC 9136) is not sent with an EVPN
nexthop: its nexthop is the gateway, which zebra resolves recursively.
Its label still holds the VNI, and bgp_is_valid_label() only checks bit 1
of the last octet, which is the low octet of the VNI. For a VNI with that
bit set, such as 2234, the gateway nexthop goes to zebra with MPLS label
VNI >> 4 (139 for 2234).

zebra keeps that label on the nexthop and on the nexthops it resolves to.
When the gateway is a host on a connected subnet, the kernel route gets
an MPLS encapsulation it should not have, and the route sent to an FPM
does too. SONiC's fpmsyncd takes a route with an encapsulation for an
EVPN route, finds no VXLAN VNI and router MAC in it and drops it, so
ROUTE_TABLE keeps whatever the prefix had before, typically the type-5
route through the VTEP that exported it. The gateway's VTEP then sends
the traffic back to that VTEP, which sends it to the gateway's VTEP
again. When the gateway is behind another VTEP, the nexthop also gets the
VXLAN encapsulation of its host route, which the FPM receives after the
MPLS one, so that case only worked by accident.

Send no label with a gateway IP nexthop: the label of an EVPN path is a
VNI and only goes with an EVPN nexthop. The route to the gateway provides
the encapsulation the gateway needs, none for a local host and the VTEP's
VNI and router MAC for a host behind another VTEP.

bgp_evpn_overlay_index_gateway_irb checks this with the FPM listener on
an L3VNI with bit 1 set: the route through a local gateway reaches the
FPM as a plain nexthop on the L2VNI SVI, and the route through a remote
gateway with the VTEP, the L3VNI and its router MAC.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Wolfe <rnwolfe@google.com>
---
 bgpd/bgp_zebra.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/bgpd/bgp_zebra.c b/bgpd/bgp_zebra.c
index 9e3f088f8d..3d588593b5 100644
--- a/bgpd/bgp_zebra.c
+++ b/bgpd/bgp_zebra.c
@@ -1444,7 +1444,14 @@ static void bgp_zebra_announce_parse_nexthop(
 		num_labels = BGP_PATH_INFO_NUM_LABELS(mpinfo);
 		labels = num_labels ? mpinfo->extra->labels->label : NULL;
 
-		if (num_labels && (is_evpn || bgp_is_valid_label(&labels[0]))) {
+		/*
+		 * The label of a path imported from EVPN is a VNI, which only
+		 * goes with an EVPN nexthop. A gateway IP overlay index nexthop
+		 * has no label: zebra resolves it through the route to the
+		 * gateway, which brings its own encapsulation if it needs one.
+		 */
+		if (num_labels &&
+		    (is_evpn || (!is_parent_evpn && bgp_is_valid_label(&labels[0])))) {
 			enum lsp_types_t nh_label_type = ZEBRA_LSP_NONE;
 
 			if (is_evpn) {
